    {
      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "8bf0b039f300a3a541a25b3ee6e84cee",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"moisture-and-precipitation-relationships\">Moisture and precipitation relationships</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Focus variables in this notebook: <code>relative_humidity_2m</code>, <code>precipitation</code>, <code>rain</code>, <code>et0_fao_evapotranspiration</code>, <code>vapour_pressure_deficit</code>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Pick one focus variable and Spark will compare it with the <strong>other 16 selected\nweather variables</strong>. The first view ranks Pearson\ncorrelations; the second lets you inspect any pair as a sampled scatterplot.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Correlation is descriptive, not causal. Weather variables are autocorrelated,\nseasonal, and often derived from each other. <code>weather_code</code> is categorical,\nwind direction is circular, and <code>is_day</code> is binary, so interpret their Pearson\ncoefficients cautiously.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
